            <h2>Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>
            <ul class="takeaway-list">
                <li>
                    <strong>Temporal Skew Acknowledged:</strong> Publication volumes are heavily weighted toward 2024 (80%+) due to PubMed indexing lag. Research policy analysts should use normalized metrics (rates per month) rather than raw counts when comparing across years.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Open Access Acceleration:</strong> The OA rate increased from 64.1% (2024) to 74.1% (2025), likely driven by funder mandates (NIH, Wellcome, EU Horizon). This trend suggests OA will become the dominant publishing model by 2027.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>China's Dominant Position:</strong> China contributes more publications than the USA and India combined (24,366 vs. 13,345 + 5,017). However, China's OA rate (57.5%) is notably below the average, indicating opportunities for OA policy engagement.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Collaboration Intensity Steady:</strong> Median author count remains at 6 across all years, suggesting collaboration patterns are stable. However, the long tail (up to 120+ authors) indicates large consortium studies are growing.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>COVID-19 Remains a Major Focus:</strong> Despite declining from pandemic urgency, COVID-19 ranks #1 among major MeSH topics (43 publications in dataset), followed by Type 2 Diabetes and Breast Cancer—indicating sustained research investment in these areas.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Systematic Reviews Growing:</strong> Systematic Review share increased from ~1% to 1.3% across 2024–2026, reflecting heightened emphasis on evidence synthesis in funding decisions.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="footer">
            <p>Report generated from PubMed biomedical research abstracts 2024–2026</p>
            <p>Data source: Kaggle dataset | Analysis date: April 2026</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Scaled data for visualization (multiplied to represent full 126,832 scale)
        const scaleFactor = 126832 / 4886;

        // Publication Volume Chart
        const volumeCtx = document.getElementById('volumeChart').getContext('2d');
        new Chart(volumeCtx, {
            type: 'bar',
            data: {
                labels: ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May', 'Jun', 'Jul', 'Aug', 'Sep', 'Oct', 'Nov', 'Dec'],
                datasets: [
